<title>Sita Swayamvaram</title>
</head>

<body bgcolor="#FF99FF">
<p><strong>Sita Swayamvaram - Kottarakkara Thampuran(1555 to 1605)</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <a href="http://www.kathakalinews.com/story.asp">BACK</a></p>
<p>This story is taken from Ramayana. Kottarakara Thampuran wrote Ramanattam (which transformed itself into Kathakali) by setting the Ramayana in eight parts. This story is one of them in which not Rama but Parasurama steals the thunder with some great performances. </p>
<p>The story is from the Bala Kanda of Ramayana. Sri Rama and his brothers 
  were growing up in Ayodhya. Rama and Lakshmana were interested in hunting in 
  forests while Bharata and Shatrughna were more interested in looking after the 
  gardens and estates attached to the palaces. At this time, Sage Vishwamitra, who got sick 
  of the troubles created by a demoness called Thataka came to visit Dasharatha. 
  Dasharatha invited Viswamitra and told him that he is ready to fulfill any wish 
  of the sage. Vishwamitra requested that the king lend him his son Rama for the 
  express purpose of destroying certain demons. Hearing this Dasharatha was 
  terrified. How can a young boy destroy demons? At this time Sage Vasishta 
  interfered and told Dasharatha to keep his word and thereby the two boys, Rama 
  and Lakshmana were sent along with Vishawmitra to the forests.</p>
<p>On the way Vishwamitra advised the boys two mantras called Bala and 
  Athibala. On Vishwamitra's orders Rama killed Thataka. Again during a yaga 
  conducted by the sage two demons, Subahu and Maricha, came to spoil the Yaga. 
  Subahu was killed while Maricha was swept away to sea by Rama's arrows. </p>
<p>Then Vishwamitra took the boys to Janaka's palace. On the way the Sage told the 
  boys the story of Ahalya who had turned into a stone due to a curse of Sage 
  Gautama. Rama gently kicks the stone shown by Vishwamatra and Ahalya comes back 
  to life and goes back to live with Gautama. Rama lifted a huge bow called 
  Thrayambaka(Shiva's bow) and broke it also, and having passed the royal test married Sita. 
  Hearing this, Dasharatha came from Ayodhya along with his other children. Hence 
  Lakshmana married Urmila, Bharatha married Mandavi, Shatrughna married 
  Suthakirti, all from Janaka's palace. </p>
<p>Now comes the climax. When the entourage was returning to Ayodhya, sage Parasurama came in their way and 
  stopped them. 'Areda Natannidunnu?' or 'Who walks?', he asks. He ridiculed Rama for breaking Shiva's bow. He said that since 
  you have broken Shiva's bow, you should at least be capable of tying my bow. 
  Rama tied the Bow easily and proceeded on his way while the defamed and 
  defeated Parasurama proceeded for doing further Tapas. </p>
<p>In this story all the royalty have pacha vesham and others are minuku. Thataka 
  is Kari and other demons are Kathi.</p>
